   3.920005205066906*^9},ExpressionUUID->"82473837-570c-4bf9-b27a-\
b88eb03e0623"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Narcissistic numbers, also known as Armstrong numbers, are a special subset \
of natural numbers. An n digit number, where n means its dimension, is \
defined as narcissistic if the sum of its digits, each raised to the power of \
n, is equal to the number itself.\n\nA well known example of a three digit \
narcissistic number (n = 3) is: 153 = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["1", "3"], "+", 
    SuperscriptBox["5", "3"], "+", 
    SuperscriptBox["3", "3"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b33b0b03-28ec-408f-89ba-6b1eafb440bc"],
 " (Note: 153 = 1 + 125 + 27)\n\nIn 1985, D. Winter mathematically proved \
that within the base 10 numeral system, narcissistic numbers can only exist \
up to a maximum length of 60 digits. This upper bound is derived from the \
following inequality constraint [1]: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "*", 
    SuperscriptBox["9", "n"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "c3a36fc4-b21a-4d05-b51c-930e4685f38a"],
 " < ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", 
    RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2529ff33-e13e-4277-9299-5a10c74004ed"],
 "\n\nFor any dimension greater than 60, the maximum possible sum of the \
powered digits ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"n", "*", 
     SuperscriptBox["9", "n"]}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "85b90af6-4a1d-45a9-baf5-15b7ac2ff14d"],
 ") is strictly less than the smallest possible number of that length ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    SuperscriptBox["10", 
     RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}]]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "69f45725-316a-4866-bb6c-342f92b0e1e8"],
 "), making the existence of larger narcissistic numbers mathematically \
impossible. Consequently, there are exactly 88 narcissistic numbers in base \
10, with the largest being the 39 digit number:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJxTTMoPSmViYGAQBmIQ/Yizauoc69eO+48HTwfRRb23orhtXzvWJDlHg2ju
4zvTQfTu+1szQHTKr6tVINoq6y+Ybik6viO17bXjI8/Zu0H0/M/TeNKA9Lzz
78D0fbWt4iA6YPUjCRDtp9uhBKJPT1ypDKI3VXRogmim3ilaYPnkDwZg9XM4
jEF0mMMLMxCtpbPQAkRX1Ry0A9GqXnt9QfSlWqEYEF2hPj8WRN/Qk2a6aPLW
cc8VLTAtpLxIp2L3W8c5WayGILq01NwURMeV1VmDaJ7v51xA9GbJ0wEgWvJJ
dhiILtr3oxBETz8xuxpEtzxaCKYBGTSXnw==
  "],ExpressionUUID->"9fa5a7f9-5403-1c4d-b45f-fc3fdf894aee"],

Cell["115,132,219,018,763,992,565,095,597,973,971,522,401", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.990602629558321*^9},
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"8f4772d4-8911-465b-8f3e-426b2a079bde"],

Cell["\<\
The primary objective of this term paper is to develop and implement the most \
efficient algorithmic approach to search for and identify as many of these 88 \
narcissistic numbers as computationally feasible.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990602648600093*^9, 
  3.990602651254992*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d95aaf50-912d-47f0-923e-\
24d2c543c5bf"],

Cell[BoxData[" "], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.694345762940171*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[10]:=",ExpressionUUID->"25190add-2924-467f-8bf7-975d689e5f07"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["4 Solution", "Section",
 PageBreakAbove->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.694341537930465*^9, 3.694341550254487*^9}, {
   3.694342355079833*^9, 3.694342363410248*^9}, 3.6943428651189747`*^9, {
   3.6943430193857985`*^9, 3.694343019923829*^9}, {3.6943431695433865`*^9, 
   3.694343174763685*^9}, 3.694345457572358*^9, 
   3.9200052107218494`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"e0ce679d-baae-4e3f-955a-\
2426a5ad958a"],

Cell["\<\
The following parameters define the dimension up to which an approach runs. \
They can be configured to test and compare the performance and speed of the \
different approaches presented in this notebook.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990602910810972*^9, 3.990602920216578*^9}, 
   3.990603048632783*^9},ExpressionUUID->"e3073dfe-8e78-4a5e-84c9-\
761da68afa0b"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"maxdimension", " ", "=", " ", "6"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990602926373199*^9, 3.9906029313684607`*^9}, {
   3.9906030565762978`*^9, 3.990603059228408*^9}, {3.990634441073742*^9, 
   3.9906344413630505`*^9}, 3.9906351027349644`*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[2]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c06122ac-92ca-4403-afe6-35506e12dbc4"],

Cell["\<\
The first step is to define a function that can check whether any given \
number is narcissistic. For this purpose, a quality function QF is defined.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9906029556147537`*^9, 
  3.990602986133419*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2a4542b8-7100-41ef-8bfa-\
879fc3c48df1"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"F", "[", "n_", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Sum", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"IntegerDigits", "[", "n", "]"}], "[", 
      RowBox[{"[", "i", "]"}], "]"}], "^", 
     RowBox[{"IntegerLength", "[", "n", "]"}]}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"i", ",", "1", ",", 
      RowBox[{"IntegerLength", "[", "n", "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990602988918713*^9, 3.990602990538207*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[3]:=",ExpressionUUID->"21918002-d0ae-43bb-8076-33213f413584"],

Cell["\<\
The quality function is derived by subtracting the digit power sum of a \
number from the number itself. Squaring this ensures that the function yields \
only positive values. Now narcissistic numbers represent not only the roots \
(zero) but also the global minima of the quality function.

In optimization theory, this squared approach acts like a quadratic penalty \
function. The squaring mechanism penalizes larger deviations from the target \
value.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9906030027311487`*^9, 3.990603009987138*^9}, {
  3.990603285216754*^9, 
  3.9906032916755667`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"145e6919-1a2a-478d-bd6e-\
f73bcbb3466a"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"QF", "[", "n_", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"n", "-", 
     RowBox[{"Sum", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"IntegerDigits", "[", "n", "]"}], "[", 
         RowBox[{"[", "i", "]"}], "]"}], "^", 
        RowBox[{"IntegerLength", "[", "n", "]"}]}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"i", ",", "1", ",", 
         RowBox[{"IntegerLength", "[", "n", "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ")"}], 
   "^", "2"}]}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9906030230811787`*^9, 3.990603024817245*^9}, {
  3.990603329002846*^9, 3.9906033455981693`*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[4]:=",ExpressionUUID->"0b006e90-21a4-46f0-9036-ab628dc44e89"],

Cell["For all narcissistic numbers: QF(n) = 0", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990603033286065*^9, 3.9906030357452183`*^9}, {
  3.99060308940854*^9, 
  3.99060309283436*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ea36391f-3960-4c72-be9b-\
89fa613c24bd"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"QF", "[", "153", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990603099553699*^9, 3.9906031068674726`*^9}, {
  3.990604252896147*^9, 3.990604256050068*^9}},
